SECTIONS = [
("H1", "Materials"),
("P", "Both experiments used the same video materials. Before the child data were analyzed, the materials were characterized in three ways: whether the videos elicited the intended V1s, how adults rated the relation between each action and its target result, and how often the corresponding RVCs occur in a corpus. Throughout, we distinguish the depicted events from the expressions used to describe them. An *action* is what the actor does in a video (e.g., blowing on a candle), and the *target result* is the change in the object that the action may or may not bring about (the candle going out). A *V1* is the monomorphemic verb that names the action (*chuī* \"blow\"), *V2* is the verb or adjective that names the target result (*miè* \"extinguished\"), and the *RVC* combines the two (*chuī-miè* \"blow-extinguished\"). Action–result association is a property of the relation between an action and a result; the adult ratings were collected for V1–object expressions, which name the action and the object it is performed on. For brevity, a *stronger-association V1* is the V1 of the expression whose action adults rated as more strongly associated with the target result, and a *weaker-association V1* is the V1 of its partner."),

("H2", "Action Pairs and Videos"),
("P", "The materials comprise 10 pairs of actions (Table 1). The two actions in each pair are performed on the same object and can bring about the same target result, but they were expected to differ in how strongly they are associated with that result: Peeling and squeezing a lychee can both get it open, and blowing on and waving at a candle can both put it out. Each action is named by a different V1, and both V1s combine with the same V2. Each pair therefore corresponds to two V1–object expressions (e.g., *bāo lìzhī* \"peel (the) lychee\" and *niē lìzhī* \"squeeze (the) lychee\") and two RVCs (*bāo-kāi* \"peel-open\" and *niē-kāi* \"squeeze-open\"). The 20 expressions form two sets of 10: the expected stronger-association expressions and their expected weaker-association partners. The V1s were originally selected as Strong and Weak V1s with the preverbal time-span adverbial diagnostic (Martin et al., 2023).⟦待核：0507 稿称部分 V1 直接取自 Martin et al. (2020)；参考文献年份需统一⟧ In the present study, the two sets are characterized by the adult ratings described below rather than by this diagnostic."),
("TABLE1", ""),
("P", "Each of the 20 actions was filmed in a full-result and a zero-result version, yielding 40 critical videos (5.0–25.0 s, *M* = 11.4 s). In the full-result version, the action brings about the target result (e.g., the lychee is peeled open); in the zero-result version, the actor performs the same action, but the object is left perceptibly unchanged (e.g., the lychee remains closed). The two versions differ only in this outcome. Each video began and ended with the actor still for 1 s. Eight filler videos showed actions without an affected object (e.g., squatting, shaking one's head). ⟦Figure 1：一对 full-result 与 zero-result 视频的截图（可沿用 0507 稿 Figures 1–2）⟧"),

("H2", "Verb–Video Correspondence"),
("P", "Because each expression was paired with its own video, the comparison between the two members of a pair is also a comparison between two videos. A norming study therefore assessed whether each video elicited the intended V1. Sixteen adults (20–24 years, *M* = 22.4; 13 female) watched the 20 zero-result videos with the final 30% removed, so that their descriptions would focus on the action rather than the outcome. Each participant saw the 10 videos of one set and described each in a full sentence. If the intended V1 did not appear, the participant was asked for another description; if it still did not appear after two attempts, the participant rated how natural the intended V1 was as a description of the action (1 = *very unnatural*, 5 = *very natural*). The intended V1 appeared in 62% of first descriptions and in 72% of all descriptions; when it did not appear, it was rated as natural (*M* = 4.32, *SD* = 0.79, range = 2.67–5). ⟦逐项数值放附录⟧ These item-level values are used as a covariate in a sensitivity analysis of Experiment 1."),

("H2", "Adult Ratings of Result Likelihood and Action Typicality"),
("P", "Twenty native speakers of Mandarin (21–35 years, *M* = 28.2) ⟦性别与招募方式待补⟧ rated each of the 20 V1–object expressions in two separate blocks. In the likelihood block, they judged how likely the target result was to follow the action named by the expression, on a scale from 0 to 100. In the typicality block, they judged how typical the action was as a means of bringing about that result, on a scale from 1 to 7. ⟦指导语、量表两端标签及一个题目的原文和译文待补⟧ Because the ratings were collected for sentences naming the expressions, they characterize the action–result relation that each expression describes, not the particular videos. The order of the two blocks was counterbalanced across four lists, which limits, but does not remove, carryover from one judgment to the other. All raters completed all items, and no ratings were excluded."),
("P", "For each rater and pair, we computed the difference between the ratings of the two expressions (expected stronger- minus expected weaker-association expression) and modeled these differences with crossed random intercepts for raters and pairs, estimated by restricted maximum likelihood. Because there were only 10 pairs, we report 95% confidence intervals from a bootstrap that resampled both raters and pairs, and exact *p* values from a randomization test that exchanged the two expression labels within pairs across all 2^10 assignments. Across the 10 pairs, the expected stronger-association expressions received higher likelihood ratings (mean difference = 33.06 points, 95% CI [21.74, 44.70], *p* = .003) and higher typicality ratings (mean difference = 2.45 points, 95% CI [1.25, 3.59], *p* = .007). The pair-level differences in the two ratings were correlated, *r* = .79, permutation *p* = .005. Mean ratings for each expression are shown in Table 1."),

("H2", "Pair Selection"),
("P", "A pair was retained for the analyses of Experiment 1 if the expected stronger-association expression received reliably higher ratings than its partner on both scales, as assessed by a within-rater label-exchange test for each pair (*p* < .05, uncorrected). Eight pairs met this criterion. Two did not. In Pair 7 (*qiā* \"pinch\" vs. *jiào* \"call\" a person, with the target result *xǐng* \"awake\"), the likelihood difference was in the expected direction, but the typicality difference was reversed (−1.00 points, 95% CI [−1.70, −0.30]). In Pair 8 (*tú* \"paint\" vs. *náo* \"scratch\" a palm, with the target result *hóng* \"red\"), neither difference was reliable (likelihood: 16.40 points, 95% CI [−1.00, 33.40]; typicality: 0.45 points, 95% CI [−0.90, 1.85]). Because action–result association is defined by the two ratings jointly, the actions in these pairs cannot be assigned to the two sets. The two pairs remained in the experimental lists, so all participants saw all 10 pairs, but their trials are excluded from the analyses of Experiment 1. Analyses with all 10 pairs are reported as a sensitivity check. The selection criterion was applied to the rating data alone. ⟦待核：确认剔除决定早于按八对方案分析儿童数据⟧"),
("P", "In the eight retained pairs, the stronger-association expressions had a mean likelihood rating of 80.08 and a mean typicality rating of 6.34, compared with 42.59 and 3.21 for their weaker-association partners. Within pairs, the likelihood difference ranged from 28.90 to 67.90 points and the typicality difference from 1.30 to 4.60 points. The pair-level tests used for selection are exploratory and uncorrected for multiple comparisons. They identify pairs in which the two ratings agree; they do not establish lexical classes of V1s."),

("H2", "Corpus Frequency of the Corresponding RVCs"),
("P", "To describe how conventional each RVC is, we searched the modern Chinese section of the CCL corpus (Zhan et al., 2019) for each of the 20 RVCs. The corpus reports the number of documents in which a string occurs rather than the number of tokens, and string matching also returns sequences in which the two characters belong to different words (e.g., *zhǐhuī | mièhuǒ* \"command | fire-fighting\" for *huī-miè*). All matches for the less frequent RVCs were therefore checked by hand. For the more frequent RVCs, matches were segmented automatically to check for word boundaries before V1 and after V2, and 10–12 matches per RVC were checked by hand; for *tú-hóng* \"paint-red,\" a sample of 60 matches was checked. Figurative and idiomatic uses (e.g., *sī-pò liǎn* \"tear-torn face,\" \"fall out with someone\") were counted, because they also indicate that the combination is conventional. For the RVCs checked by sampling, the number of documents was estimated by multiplying the raw document count by the proportion of checked matches that were resultative uses; for the other RVCs, all matches were checked. Document counts are shown in Table 1."),
("P", "In seven of the eight retained pairs, the RVC formed with the stronger-association V1 occurred in more documents than the RVC formed with its weaker-association partner, in most pairs by more than an order of magnitude. The exception was Pair 6, in which *tuī-dǎo* \"push-fallen\" (5,750 documents) was far more frequent than *tī-dǎo* \"kick-fallen\" (480), largely because of abstract uses such as *tuīdǎo chónglái* \"tear down and start over.\" The pattern does not reflect the frequency of the V1s themselves: As single words, several weaker-association V1s (e.g., *pāi* \"slap,\" *àn* \"press,\" *tuī* \"push,\" *chōng* \"rinse\") are more frequent than their stronger-association partners (Speer, 2022). Most weaker-association RVCs were attested, although rarely; only *huī-miè* \"wave-extinguished\" was unattested in its resultative use. The corpus data thus indicate that the RVCs formed with stronger-association V1s are more conventional, not that those formed with weaker-association V1s are unacceptable. Because the CCL corpus consists mainly of written texts, the counts do not describe children's input. RVC frequency is reported to characterize the materials and is not entered into the analyses."),

("H1", "Experiment 1: Comprehension"),
("H2", "Method"),
("H3", "Participants"),
("P", "Participants were 32 four-year-olds (14 female; ⟦age range in y;m, M⟧), 32 five-year-olds (15 female; ⟦age range in y;m, M⟧), and 40 adults (18 female; 18.3–37.2 years, *M* = 23.1). Children were recruited from two kindergartens in Hunan Province, China, and adults from a university in Beijing. All participants were native speakers of Mandarin with no reported language or intellectual impairments. Guardians and adult participants gave written informed consent under a protocol approved by the university's institutional review board. Children received a book as a gift, and adults received 10 RMB. Sample size was set before data collection at 32 children per age group, following previous studies of Mandarin verb comprehension (Chen, 2017; J. Liu, 2018)."),
("H3", "Design and Materials"),
("P", "On each trial, participants watched one critical video and answered a perfective yes–no question about it. The question contained either the RVC or the V1, with the object as a bare noun, as in (3)."),
("EX", [("(3)", "a.", "*Tā bāo-le lìzhī ma?*"), ("", "", "she peel-PERF lychee Q"), ("", "", "\"Did she peel (the) lychee?\""),
        ("", "b.", "*Tā bāo-kāi-le lìzhī ma?*"), ("", "", "she peel-open-PERF lychee Q"), ("", "", "\"Did she peel (the) lychee open?\"")]),
("P", "Form (RVC vs. V1) and set (stronger vs. weaker association) were manipulated between participants, and result realization (full vs. zero) within participants. Eight lists crossed four event lists with the two forms. Each event list contained one action from each of the 10 pairs, all from the same set, with five videos in their full-result and five in their zero-result version; across the two event lists for each set, each video appeared once in each version. Each list also contained eight filler videos (one practice and seven test trials), whose questions either matched or did not match the depicted action, to balance \"yes\" and \"no\" answers. Participants were randomly assigned to lists. In the eight retained pairs, each participant judged eight critical trials, and across lists each retained video appeared equally often in its full-result and zero-result version."),
("P", "With about four children per age group and five adults per list, the V1 descriptions of each set were judged by eight children per age group and 10 adults, and each V1 description in a given result condition by about four children per age group. Comparisons between the two sets are therefore largely between participants, and item-level estimates are imprecise."),
("H3", "Procedure"),
("P", "Children were tested individually in a quiet room in their kindergarten. After each video, they heard the question and answered orally, and an experimenter recorded the response. If a child reported not having seen the video clearly or did not answer, the video was played once more. Adults were tested online in Gorilla (Anwyl-Irvine et al., 2020); they received oral instructions from an experimenter and selected their answers themselves. Each session began with one practice trial. Acceptance was coded 1 and rejection 0."),
("H2", "Analysis Plan"),
("P", "Trial-level acceptance is modeled with Bayesian logistic mixed-effects models in brms (Bürkner, 2017) in R (R Core Team, 2024). Bayesian estimation was chosen because near-ceiling acceptance in full-result events and in some adult conditions produces quasi-separation, and frequentist models with the required interaction structure did not converge on these data. Factors are sum-coded. Fixed effects have Normal(0, 1.5) priors, standard deviations Exponential(1) priors, and correlations LKJ(2) priors. Convergence is assessed by *R̂* < 1.01 and adequate effective sample sizes. We report posterior medians, 95% credible intervals, and predicted acceptance probabilities, and, for directional predictions, the posterior probability that the effect has the predicted sign. Because precision in designs with few stimuli depends mainly on the number of stimuli (Westfall et al., 2014), an interval that includes both zero and sizable effects is treated as inconclusive rather than as evidence of no difference."),
("H3", "RVC and V1 Descriptions"),
("P", "The first model addresses RQ1. It includes form (RVC vs. V1), result realization (full vs. zero), age group (4-year-olds, 5-year-olds, adults), and their interactions; V1 trials from the two sets are pooled. Random effects are intercepts for participants, items (a V1–object expression and its RVC), and pairs; a by-participant slope for result realization; and by-item slopes for form, result realization, and age group. The focal contrasts are the full–zero difference in acceptance for each form in each age group and the difference between these two differences (Form × Result Realization), which indicates whether result realization affects acceptance of the two forms differently. Comparing this interaction between children and adults and between 4- and 5-year-olds addresses the age questions in RQ1."),
("H3", "Stronger- and Weaker-Association V1s"),
("P", "The second model addresses RQ2 and uses V1 trials only. It includes set (stronger vs. weaker association), result realization, age group, and their interactions. Random effects are intercepts for participants, items, and pairs; a by-participant slope for result realization; by-item slopes for result realization and age group; and a by-pair slope for set, which allows the difference between the two V1s of a pair to vary across pairs. The focal estimates are (a) the difference between sets in zero-result events in each age group; (b) the Set × Result Realization interaction, which assesses whether this difference is larger in zero-result than in full-result events; and (c) the difference between children and adults in the zero-result difference between sets. Because adult acceptance in zero-result events may approach ceiling, the child–adult comparison is evaluated on the log-odds scale, and predicted probabilities are reported alongside."),
("H3", "Sensitivity Analyses"),
("P", "Three analyses assess whether the conclusions depend on the materials: (a) refitting both models with all 10 pairs, with the actions of Pairs 7 and 8 assigned to sets as in the original design; (b) adding the item-level verb–video correspondence values as a covariate; and (c) refitting the models with each pair left out in turn. Frequentist refits are reported where they converge. These analyses are not used to select a preferred subset of items. Acceptance of each V1 and RVC description is also reported by result realization and age group, to show whether a difference between sets is carried by most pairs or by a few."),
("H2", "Results"),
("P", "Accuracy on filler trials was 100% for adults, 97% for 4-year-olds, and 95% for 5-year-olds, and no participant was excluded. The data comprise 1,040 critical responses, of which 832 belong to the eight retained pairs (416 RVC and 416 V1 trials)."),
("Q", "⟦待补：按八对、两组方案重新分析后报告。0507 稿的结果基于全部 10 对和 Strong/Weak 编码，不能直接沿用。⟧"),

("H1", "Experiment 2: Production"),
("H2", "Method"),
("H3", "Participants"),
("P", "A new sample of 32 four-year-olds (14 female; ⟦age range in y;m, M⟧), 32 five-year-olds (18 female; ⟦age range in y;m, M⟧), and 40 adults (29 female; 20.8–33.8 years, *M* = 24.3) was recruited from the same populations as in Experiment 1, with the same consent procedure and compensation."),
("H3", "Materials and Procedure"),
("P", "The 40 critical videos were divided into four lists. Each list contained the 10 actions of one set, five in their full-result and five in their zero-result version, together with the eight filler videos (one practice and seven test trials). Four further lists presented the same videos in reverse order, yielding eight lists. Participants were randomly assigned to lists, watched each video, and were asked to describe what happened. ⟦指导语原文⟧ Children responded orally, and an experimenter transcribed their responses; adults typed their descriptions online. Because response modality differed between children and adults, child–adult differences are interpreted with caution."),
("P", "All 10 pairs are included in the analyses of Experiment 2, because these analyses compare result conditions and do not require the actions to be assigned to sets. Analyses restricted to the eight retained pairs are reported as a sensitivity check. ⟦请确认 Exp 2 用全部 10 对⟧"),
("H3", "Coding"),
("P", "The coding unit is the complete response to a video, including information expressed across clauses. Responses were recoded from the transcripts for the present analyses. ⟦第二名编码员及一致性待补⟧ Nonresponses and responses without a verb are excluded and reported."),
("P4", ("Form.", "Each response is coded for whether it contains an RVC, affirmed or negated (e.g., *bāo-kāi-le* \"peeled open\"; *méi bāo-kāi* \"did not peel open\"). Responses without an RVC contain only monomorphemic predicates, which may name the action (e.g., *bāo* \"peel\") or the result (e.g., *miè-le* \"went out\"). Serial-verb responses are retained and coded in the same way.")),
("P4", ("Information.", "Each response is also coded for whether it mentions the action and whether it mentions the target result. The action is coded as mentioned when any verb describes the depicted action; whether this verb is the intended V1 is recorded separately, and general verbs such as *nòng* \"do, handle\" are coded as mentioning the action without specifying it. The target result is coded as mentioned when the response contains the V2 or an expression listed as equivalent for that item in the coding manual, whether the result is affirmed or denied. Only explicitly expressed information is coded: A *bǎ* construction with a monomorphemic verb (e.g., *bǎ làzhú chuī-le* \"BA candle blow-PERF\") is coded as mentioning the action only. ⟦注意：0507 稿把 bǎ 句中的谓语一律编为 RVC，这里改了⟧ Whether a mentioned result is affirmed or denied is recorded, so that responses affirming the target result in a zero-result event can be identified.")),
("P4", ("Response categories.", "Combining the two codes, each response is assigned to one of five categories: action only, target result only, action and target result in an RVC, action and target result in separate predicates, or neither. The RVC and action-only categories correspond to the RVC and V1 descriptions judged in Experiment 1.")),
("H2", "Analysis Plan"),
("P", "Two binary outcomes are modeled with the Bayesian specification of Experiment 1: whether a response contains an RVC and whether it mentions the target result. Each model includes result realization, age group, and their interaction, with random intercepts for participants, items (the videos of one action), and pairs; a by-participant slope for result realization; and by-item slopes for result realization and age group. Because negated RVCs are counted as RVCs, the first outcome concerns the form of the description regardless of whether the result is affirmed or denied. Because every RVC mentions both the action and the target result, the two outcomes overlap by design; comparing them shows whether fewer RVCs in zero-result events reflect fewer mentions of the result or a shift to other ways of mentioning it. Among responses without an RVC, the proportions of action-only, result-only, and separate-predicate responses are reported by result realization and age group and modeled if cell sizes permit. ⟦待定：多分类模型还是仅描述⟧ Responses that affirm the target result in zero-result events are counted by age group. The adult ratings are not used in these analyses."),
("H2", "Results"),
("Q", "⟦待补：按新的编码方案（完整回答为单位）重新编码并分析后报告。⟧"),

("H1", "General Discussion"),
("Q", "⟦本节为计划稿，将根据结果修改。⟧"),
("P", "The study asks three questions about how Mandarin-speaking 4- and 5-year-olds interpret and describe events in which an action does or does not bring about a target result. First, do children evaluate RVC and V1 descriptions differently with respect to the result, and do 4-year-olds already do so? Second, with the object and the result held constant, do children judge V1 descriptions differently depending on how strongly the named action is associated with the result? Third, how does the realization of the result shape which forms children use and whether they mention the result in their own descriptions?"),
("H2", "RVC and V1 Descriptions"),
("P", "A larger effect of result realization on RVC than on V1 descriptions would show that children treat a result expressed by V2 differently from a result that is only associated with the action named by V1. Previous studies have shown this distinction in 5-year-olds (J. Liu et al., 2026) and in a small group of children around 4;6 (Chen, 2017). If 4-year-olds show the same interaction as 5-year-olds, the distinction is in place by 4 years of age for the events tested here; if the interaction is weaker in 4-year-olds, it continues to develop between 4 and 5. In either case, the conclusion concerns the selected RVCs and events, not RVC semantics or telicity in general. A smaller difference between the two forms in children than in adults would replicate the finding that children judge V1 descriptions of events without the result more strictly than adults do (Chen, 2017; J. Liu et al., 2026)."),
("H2", "Action–Result Association and V1 Descriptions"),
("P", "If children reject V1 descriptions of zero-result events more often when the V1 names an action strongly associated with the target result, their stricter judgments are not uniform across V1s but vary with the relation between the depicted action and the result. Chen (2017) found differences among V1s, but each V1 was presented with a different object and result. Because the present design holds the object and the target result constant within pairs, a difference between sets cannot be attributed to differences between objects or results. It would show that, for children, what a V1 description conveys about a result depends on how the named action relates to that result. A difference between sets in adults would show the same for mature interpretation, consistent with the cline in adults' acceptance of result cancellation (Chen, 2018) and with evidence that comprehenders' expectations about events depend on the combination of a verb with its arguments (Bicknell et al., 2010; McRae & Matsuki, 2009)."),
("P", "A larger difference between sets in children than in adults would locate children's stricter judgments mainly in V1s that name strongly associated actions. This pattern is compatible with three explanations that the binary task cannot separate: Children may find it harder than adults to set aside an inference that the result occurred, may reject a true description as inadequate because it leaves an expected result unaddressed, or may judge a failed attempt to be a poor instance of the action. The pragmatic explanation fits less naturally with a larger difference in children, because children tend to be more tolerant than adults of underinformative descriptions in binary judgment tasks (Katsos & Bishop, 2011). Because adult acceptance in zero-result events may approach ceiling, however, a larger child difference on the probability scale could partly reflect the adult ceiling, which is why the comparison is evaluated on the log-odds scale."),
("P", "Because the stronger- and weaker-association expressions differ in both rated likelihood and rated typicality, the study cannot determine which dimension, or what the two share, underlies a difference between sets; the two ratings diverged only in Pair 7, which was excluded. The difference between sets also goes together with a difference in the conventionality of the corresponding RVCs. A child who often hears *chuī-miè* \"blow-extinguished\" may come to expect a candle to go out after *chuī* \"blow,\" so familiarity with the RVC offers an alternative account of a difference between sets. This account is not incompatible with the association account, because an action that reliably brings about a result is also likely to be described often with the corresponding RVC, but the present materials cannot separate the two."),
("H2", "Form and Information in Production"),
("P", "Comparing RVC use with mention of the target result separates the forms children use from the information they express. If both decline in zero-result events, speakers describing a failed attempt tend to leave the result unmentioned rather than express it in another form. If children mention the result without an RVC more often than adults, for example by naming the result alone or in a separate predicate, their lower RVC use reflects how they package the information rather than less attention to the result. In either case, RVC frequency alone cannot show whether children encode a result. Mentions of the result in zero-result events, typically negated as in *méi bāo-kāi* \"did not peel open,\" indicate that the speaker treated the absent result as relevant to the event, and responses that affirm the target result in a zero-result event would indicate that a speaker described an unattained result as attained. Because the two experiments tested independent samples, links between comprehension and production hold at the group level only."),
("H2", "Limitations and Future Directions"),
("P", "Several limitations follow from the design. First, the yes–no task cannot distinguish a judgment that a V1 description is false from a judgment that it is true but inadequate; a graded or ternary response format (Katsos & Bishop, 2011) or a cancellation task would be needed to separate them. Second, each V1 appears with a single object and result, so the data cannot show that the same V1 conveys more or less about a result depending on the result in question; presenting the same V1 with different objects and results, such as *tī* \"kick\" with a bottle and with a door, would test this directly. Third, likelihood, typicality, and RVC conventionality covary across the retained pairs. Separating them would require pairs in which they diverge, as in the excluded Pair 7, with the two ratings collected from different raters. Fourth, with eight pairs and sets assigned between participants, each difference between sets rests on few items and small groups, and item-level estimates are descriptive. Fifth, the ratings describe expressions rather than videos, and the videos did not always elicit the intended V1. Finally, children and adults were tested in different modalities, and comprehension and production were tested in independent samples."),

("H1", "Conclusion"),
("P", "This study examines how Mandarin-speaking 4- and 5-year-olds interpret and describe events in which an action does or does not bring about a target result. Comparing RVC and V1 descriptions tests whether children distinguish a result expressed by V2 from one that is only associated with the action named by V1. Comparing V1s that name actions on the same object, but differ in how strongly those actions are associated with the same result, tests whether children's interpretation of V1 descriptions varies with the relation between the action and the result rather than with the verb alone. Comparing RVC use with mention of the result in production asks whether children who use fewer RVCs also leave the result unexpressed. ⟦结论待根据结果改写⟧"),
]

